is medal, at the age of 17 years and 97 days, (after " [:a {:href "/athletes/2723"} "Jennifer Capriati"] ", who won Olympic gold at the women’s singles event in 1992, at the age of 16 years and 132 days). The 2024 top seeds were the US American pair of " [:a {:href "/athletes/154963"} "Coco Gauff"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/147287"} "Jessica Pegula"] ", but they were eliminated in the second round by Czechia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/154911"} "Karolína Muchová"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/154912"} "Linda Nosková"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the final, Andreyeva and Shnayder lost to Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/117604"} "Sara Errani"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/144108"} "Jasmine Paolini"] ". It was Italy’s first Olympic gold medal in tennis, and Errani completed the career Golden Slam, becoming the seventh woman to do so, after the Williams sisters (" [:a {:href "/athletes/94424"} "Serena"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/94425"} "Venus"] "), " [:a {:href "/athletes/2740"} "Pam Shriver"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/2727"} "Gigi Fernandez"] ", Barbora Krejčíková, and Kateřina Siniaková. At the age of 37, Errani became the oldest player to win an Olympic tennis gold medal."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the bronze medal match, Spain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/154917"} "Cristina Bucșa"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/142455"} "Sara Sorribes"] " defeated Czechia’s Karolína Muchová and Linda Nosková. It was Spain’s first Olympic medal in women’s tennis since 2008."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/117604"} "Sara Errani"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/144108"} "Jasmine Paolini"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td